MacBookPro13,2 Ubuntu 26.04 LTS support, v0.5.0 beta
====================================================

This package extends the single-user-tested v0.4.0 integration with:
- APT post-transaction read-only DKMS verification and a boot health report;
- an APT::NeverAutoRemove rule protecting kernel 7.0.0-30-generic (the one
  successfully tested with all core hardware on a real MacBookPro13,2);
- an optional repair command, using installed DKMS source, that refuses
  to build if the exact matching Ubuntu patched kernel source is unavailable;
- a fix for v0.4's end-of-script unbound-local-variable cleanup error;
- immutable Git SHA pins for the three upstream code and firmware snapshots.

The upgrade from v0.4.0 automatically sets up monitoring and protects the
working kernel if it is present. It DOES NOT reinstall working drivers or
change EFI, GRUB, suspend, Thunderbolt or partition configuration.

IMPORTANT: "DKMS installed" means only that a driver built and was installed.
It DOES NOT guarantee that newer kernels safely initialize the audio codec,
Touch Bar or Wi-Fi at runtime. The audio driver patches Ubuntu's kernel HDA
interfaces and its kernel 7 upstream patch is still unmerged. Kernels after
7.0.0-30-generic must be tested. APT will install new kernels normally and
may boot them by default. If problems occur, boot the preserved 7.0.0-30
fallback from GRUB's Advanced options (if GRUB provides that menu). It is
not a promise of automatic rollback. The user must still back up data.

Existing v0.4 users:
  Install v0.5 .deb. There is NO need to run 'install' again.
  Check only if desired: mbp132-support status

Fresh Ubuntu 26.04 users (MacBookPro13,2 only):
  sudo mbp132-support install
  Reboot, then check Wi-Fi, sound, microphone, camera and Touch Bar.

If a supported 7.0.0-generic kernel reports missing DKMS modules:
  sudo mbp132-support repair

Never force 'repair' if a new kernel has boot or runtime problems: instead
boot the known-good version. Versions outside the 7.0 kernel family are
explicitly flagged as unsupported, not patched automatically.

What changes:
  - Sources under /usr/src/ (DKMS), /var/lib/mbp132-support/ (backups)
  - /lib/firmware/brcm/brcmfmac43602-pcie.txt (community Wi-Fi NVRAM)
  - APT monitoring in /etc/apt/apt.conf.d/, one generated fallback rule
  - A boot status service and logs (read-only checks)
  - /etc/modprobe.d/, /etc/modules-load.d/, /etc/udev/rules.d/
  - Initramfs rebuilt for the targeted kernel at original driver install

No automatic module recompilation logic is reinvented: Ubuntu's DKMS kernel
hooks handle normal automatic rebuilds when the package's dkms.conf
AUTOINSTALL is enabled and matching headers/sources are installed.

Attribution (upstream sources fetched ONLY at exact immutable commits):
  Audio: duzelli/snd_hda_macbookpro, commit
     3414707841e7a518707be4748cd8787794e19045
     PR against davidjo/snd_hda_macbookpro (#216); license upstream.
  Touch Bar: AJ-dev-i60/t1-touchbar, commit
     20d65c7b0fe6d05ea9734f869b27384a62de5109
     driver GPL-2.0; installer/docs as described upstream.
  Wi-Fi: DeyAgrO/macbook-pro-13-2-linux, commit
     c005707321186f6ac6ac81fbe577226189568f69
     community-distributed NVRAM derived from Apple hardware. Check licensing
     and applicable RF compliance before redistributing firmware.

This is a community integrator, not endorsed by Ubuntu or Apple. GPL and
upstream licensing must be respected in any redistribution. It has been
structurally tested off-device but v0.5 update behavior is not yet hardware-
validated. The first end-to-end MacBookPro13,2 hardware success was with v0.4
on Ubuntu 26.04.1, kernel 7.0.0-30-generic.
